        <p class="lede">
          Protein powder is the better choice for muscle, because collagen peptides are an incomplete protein with no tryptophan and very little leucine; in a 2020 trial, whey raised muscle protein synthesis and the same dose of collagen did not. Collagen still has a case for joints, and a contested one for skin. Below: the evidence, daily protein targets, taking both, and cost per 25 g.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Collagen vs Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein powder wins for muscle, and collagen is a different supplement with a narrower job. Whey, casein, soy and good plant blends are complete proteins: they supply all nine essential amino acids and enough leucine to switch on muscle protein synthesis. Collagen peptides have no tryptophan and only about a third of whey's leucine, so gram for gram they do far less for muscle. Collagen's case rests on joints and connective tissue, where the evidence is modest, and on skin, where it is disputed.
          </p>

