        <div id="take-both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Can You Take Collagen and Protein Powder Together?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, you can take collagen and protein powder together, and doing so is safe for healthy adults. Many people stir collagen into morning coffee and drink a whey or plant shake after training. You can even mix a scoop of unflavored collagen into the protein shake itself; it adds 15 to 18 g of protein and 60 to 70 calories without changing the taste much.
          </p>

          <p>
            Combining them makes the most sense when each one has a clear job. The protein powder covers muscle: it gives you 25 to 30 g of complete protein with 2 to 3 g of leucine. The collagen covers connective tissue: 10 to 15 g of glycine-rich peptides, ideally with some vitamin C, taken consistently for months. Pairing them does not make collagen a better muscle protein, but adding collagen on top of an adequate complete-protein intake costs you nothing in muscle terms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing is flexible. There is no strong evidence that protein powder must be taken within a narrow window after training, and total daily protein matters more. For collagen, the only timing data come from the small gelatin study, which gave it an hour before exercise. If tendons or joints are the reason you buy it, taking it before a session is a reasonable, low-cost habit; otherwise, take it whenever you will remember.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the calories and the budget. A collagen scoop plus a protein shake adds roughly 170 to 230 calories and $3 to $4 a day at the prices we saw, which adds up if you are trying to lose fat or keep costs down. If you need to choose one, choose the complete protein. People on a protein-restricted diet for kidney disease, and anyone pregnant, breastfeeding or on medication, should check total protein and supplements with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Is Cheaper per 25 g of Protein, Collagen or Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Collagen is not reliably cheaper than protein powder per gram, and it is more expensive per gram of useful protein. At the Amazon prices we saw on 18 November 2026, Vital Proteins Collagen Peptides cost about $1.53 per 25 g of protein, Momentous Collagen Peptides about $2.89, Garden of Life Sport Plant Protein about $1.84 and Dymatize ISO100 in vanilla about $2.00.
          </p>

          <p>
            We work out cost per 25 g as price divided by total protein in the tub, times 25. For Vital Proteins, 28 servings of 18 g is 504 g of protein, so $30.85 buys about 20 portions of 25 g, or $1.53 each. For ISO100, 76 servings of 25 g is 76 portions, so $151.75 works out to $2.00. ISO100 prices vary a lot by flavor; Birthday Cake was $119.98 for 75 servings the same day, or about $1.60.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheapest collagen can look like a bargain against a premium whey isolate, but that comparison flatters collagen. Ordinary whey blends often run well under $1.50 per 25 g, and our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheapest protein powders</a> guide lists several that do. More importantly, 25 g of collagen does not deliver what 25 g of whey does for muscle. If you only counted the leucine, you would need roughly three times as much collagen as whey to match a scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certified collagen costs more. Momentous, which carries NSF Certified for Sport status per the brand, costs nearly twice as much per gram as Vital Proteins, partly because each scoop includes branded Fortigel peptides and vitamin C. That premium is worth paying for tested athletes and for people buying collagen specifically for joints, and hard to justify for anyone who just wants a few grams in their coffee.
          </p>

