        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Should You Buy: Collagen, Protein Powder or Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people should buy a protein powder first and add collagen only if they have a specific reason. A complete protein powder does the job people usually want from a supplement: it helps you build or keep muscle, reach a daily protein target and stay full. Collagen is a specialist add-on for joints and connective tissue, and its skin benefit is still in dispute.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pick collagen if...</h3>

          <p>
            You already eat enough protein from food, you have joint or tendon complaints you want to address alongside training and physical therapy, and you like the idea of an unflavored powder that disappears into coffee. Vital Proteins is the low-cost choice; Momentous is the choice if you are drug-tested or want the Fortigel dose. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">best collagen protein powders</a> guide covers marine, multi-collagen and other certified options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pick protein powder if...</h3>

          <p>
            Your goal is muscle, fat loss without muscle loss, or simply hitting a protein number you keep missing. A whey isolate like Dymatize ISO100 suits most people, including many with mild lactose intolerance, and a plant blend like Garden of Life Sport suits vegans and anyone avoiding dairy. If you are deciding between protein types, see <a href="/casein-vs-whey-protein-powder/">casein vs whey</a>; if you are thin and need calories as well as protein, see today's <a href="/mass-gainer-vs-protein-powder/">mass gainer vs protein powder</a> comparison; and if you are wondering what else belongs in the cupboard, <a href="/creatine-vs-protein-powder/">creatine vs protein powder</a> explains why creatine is the other supplement with strong evidence.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Take both if...</h3>

          <p>
            You train hard, already reach your protein target with complete sources, and want to give collagen a 12-week trial for joints or tendons. Count your protein shake toward your muscle target and treat the collagen as a bonus. Anyone with kidney disease, a history of kidney stones, a fish or shellfish allergy (for marine collagen), or who is pregnant or taking medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Swapping your protein shake for collagen coffee</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Replacing a 25 g whey or plant shake with a collagen scoop looks like an even trade on the label, but you lose most of the leucine and all the tryptophan. In the Oikawa trial, that exact swap meant muscle protein synthesis barely moved. Keep the complete protein and add collagen on top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting collagen as muscle protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen grams belong in your total, not in your complete-protein total. If your target is 130 g for muscle and 20 g comes from collagen, you are effectively short. Set the muscle target using whey, dairy, meat, eggs, soy or a plant blend, and let collagen ride along as extra protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting results in two weeks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The joint and skin trials ran for roughly 8 to 12 weeks, and the benefits they found were modest. Taking collagen for a fortnight and judging it on how your knees or skin feel tells you very little. If you try it, commit to about three months at a consistent 10 to 15 g dose.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying collagen for muscle because of one study</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 2015 sarcopenia trial compared collagen with a placebo containing no protein, so it shows that extra protein helped older men, not that collagen matches whey. Leucine-matched collagen still lost to whey for muscle size in the 2022 trial. Judge collagen on joint and tendon outcomes instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring certification if you are tested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most collagen tubs, including the best-selling Vital Proteins powder, carry no sport certification we could find. Drug-tested athletes should choose a powder listed by NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, such as Momentous Collagen, and check the specific product in the certifier's database before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is collagen better than protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not for muscle. Protein powders made from whey, casein, soy or blended plants are complete proteins with plenty of leucine, and whey beat collagen for muscle protein synthesis in a 2020 trial and for muscle growth in a 2022 trial. Collagen is only the better choice if your goal is connective tissue support and you already eat enough complete protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can collagen replace protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No, collagen cannot replace protein powder if muscle or a daily protein target is the goal. It has no tryptophan and about a third of whey's leucine, so its protein score on the PDCAAS scale is zero. Use collagen as an add-on for joints, and keep a complete protein powder, or enough meat, dairy, eggs, soy or legumes, for muscle.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does collagen count as protein intake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Collagen counts toward your total grams of protein, but not as high-quality protein. A 2019 modelling paper found that collagen could make up to 36 percent of daily protein in a typical Western diet while still meeting essential amino acid needs. The simplest rule is to hit your muscle target with complete protein and treat collagen as extra.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I mix collagen and protein powder in the same shake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Unflavored collagen peptides dissolve into a whey or plant shake without much change to taste, adding about 15 to 18 g of protein and 60 to 70 calories per scoop. It is safe for healthy adults. It doesn't make the collagen work better for muscle, so think of it as convenience rather than a boost.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is collagen good for building muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Collagen is a poor muscle builder compared with complete proteins. In older women, 30 g twice a day for six days did not raise muscle protein synthesis significantly over baseline, while the same dose of whey did. In young lifters, whey added more muscle thickness than collagen even after extra leucine was added to the collagen.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should women take collagen or protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most women benefit more from a complete protein powder, especially when lifting, dieting or over 50, because muscle retention depends on leucine-rich protein. Collagen is marketed to women for skin, but the 2025 meta-analysis found no effect in independent or high-quality trials. Collagen can be added if joint support is the goal and protein intake is already high.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is more expensive, collagen or protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Per gram, it depends on the product; per gram of useful protein, collagen is more expensive. At the prices we saw, Vital Proteins collagen cost about $1.53 per 25 g of protein and Momentous about $2.89, while Garden of Life Sport Plant cost about $1.84 and Dymatize ISO100 about $2.00. Budget whey blends are often cheaper than both.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
