        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Protein powder is the better buy for almost everyone, because it is a complete protein and collagen is not. Collagen has no tryptophan and too little leucine to compete with whey or a plant blend for muscle, and the two head-to-head trials say exactly that. Count collagen toward your total protein, never toward your muscle protein.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Collagen still earns a place for people who already eat enough protein and want something for joints and tendons, where the evidence is modest but positive. The skin case is weaker than the marketing suggests. If you buy both, pick a certified collagen like Momentous if you are tested, or a basic tub like Vital Proteins if you are not, and see our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen buyer's guide</a> for the full list.
